module stream {
  skill og-game
  doc "Asset streaming groups (phase 3): a scene's `stream \"<name>\" { asset ...; distance x y z r [unload d] | volume \"<entity>\"; priority n }` blocks name glTF models (without skins or morph targets), textures, texture arrays, flipbooks (an `array true` sheet brings its array), sounds, environment maps, palettes, looks and colour LUTs (`.cube`) that load and unload together, and what loads them: the camera within `r` of the anchor (kept until it is past `unload`, 1.25 r by default), the camera inside the named entity's box (its position ± half its scale, turned with it), or neither (og.stream.request only). `og cook` packs each group's files into `cooked/stream/<name>.ogb`; `og build --publish` ships the packs beside the boot bundle, which the shell and the service worker fetch first, and fetches a pack only when its group loads."
  doc "At every tick boundary the engine decides which groups are wanted, admits them by priority (then name) within the memory budget (256 MiB by default; GPU textures and meshes and decoded audio, as the cook measured them), evicting lower-priority resident groups to make room for a higher one, and reads and puts in place at most one group's assets per boundary. A group that cannot fit waits, queued; one larger than the whole budget fails. Until its group is resident, a model draws its proxy (its coarsest LOD, untextured), a texture is a 1x1 grey placeholder and a sound plays as silence of its own length; then the real ones take over the same handles. Physics always collides with the proxy's meshes and a sound's length never changes, so nothing a tick reads depends on what is resident: a run's world hash is the same streamed or not."
  doc "Texture arrays, flipbooks, environment maps, palettes, looks and colour LUTs have no stand-in: they load once their group is resident, and when it unloads the renderer lets them go and their handles wait (the sky lights the scene, Stylise.palette quantises to none, PostEffects.lut grades nothing, og.render.sprite of the array and applying the look are refused, an og.Sprite, a flipbook's sprite or a particle emitter of the array draws nothing) until the group is resident again, when they come back under the same handles. One the frame still uses when its group unloads (the map Environment lights it with, the palette Stylise quantises to, an array a sprite, a screen sprite or a particle emitter draws from, the look last applied, the LUT PostEffects grades with) is never freed under the frame: it is kept, with a warning naming it in the engine's log, until the frame stops using it. What is kept still takes its memory: its bytes count in the budget's `used` (and `kept`, and its group's StreamStatus.kept) until it is let go, so a group that would not fit beside it waits."
  doc "Every change of a group's state sends StreamEvent at the start of the next tick; og.stream.status and og.stream.budget answer at once, and the probe's `dump_stream` lists the budget, the memory used and its peak, every group (trigger, state, why, since which tick, what it keeps for the frame after unloading with each asset's bytes), the recent changes, the terrain streamer's tiles and the fetch queue."
  doc "What streams is read through one queue: first the chunks of the sounds cooked in chunks that a playhead needs soonest (the one it is playing, then by the seconds until it gets there at its pitch, a loop's start by the time to its wrap), then the loading groups' packs by priority (then name). At most four reads are in flight at once (on the web, fetches; natively a read completes at once, so only the order shows); the rest wait in that order. `dump_stream`'s `fetch` lists what is in flight and what waits, each with its path and what it is for (a sound's chunk and its seconds, a group's pack and its priority)."

  doc "World cells (G3-3) stream entities, not assets: an entity with an og.StreamCell and a sub-scene (a `scene \"<id>\"` line in its scene block, or StreamCell.scene from og.load.scene) is a cell. Its sub-scene's entities, and with them their colliders, bodies and everything else they carry, spawn when it loads, placed by the cell's transform (its top-level entities are roots, at the cell's transform after their own, so physics bodies among them are where they look) and each with an og.CellMember naming the cell; when it unloads, every member and its descendants are despawned. Whether a cell is loaded is decided as each tick starts, before any system runs, from world state alone: it loads while an og.StreamAnchor (a player, a vehicle) is within StreamCell.load of its box, unloads once every anchor is past StreamCell.unload, and stays loaded while StreamCell.pin is set. Without any anchor the og.Camera resource's position anchors, a convenience for a single-player game; a lockstep or rollback session (og.net) refuses that, since each peer's camera is its own. Cells load in entity id order and a sub-scene's entities spawn in its text order, so the same world spawns the same entity ids on both hosts, in a replay, a rollback and after a restore. Nothing about a cell waits on a download: the sub-scene's blob is in the boot bundle, and its models and textures draw their stand-ins until their streaming group is resident."
  doc "A cell's state is world state: StreamCell.loaded, and its sub-scene's entities, are in snapshots, saves and the world hash, so restoring a save restores which cells are loaded and what is in them; an unloaded cell's entities are not in the world and come back from its sub-scene, as authored, when it loads again. Each change sends CellEvent in the tick it happens, and add hooks and `added` filters on the sub-scene's components see its entities arrive. The probe's `dump_stream` lists every cell (`cells`): its entity, sub-scene, state, entities and the ticks it last loaded and unloaded."

  component StreamCell {
    doc "A world cell: loads its sub-scene's entities while an og.StreamAnchor is near its box, and despawns them when none is (the module's doc). The box is the entity's world position ± half `size`, turned with it; a zero size is a point."
    field scene Asset "The sub-scene it loads: set from the entity's `scene \"<id>\"` line when its scene spawns, or og.load.scene's handle for a cell spawned by game code. 0: none (the cell never loads)."
    field size Vector3 "The box's full extents, in metres along the entity's axes (its scale does not apply). Zero: a point at the entity's position." {
      unit m
    }
    field load f32 "Loads while an anchor is within this distance of the box (0: only while one is inside it)." {
      unit m
      min 0
    }
    field unload f32 "Unloads once every anchor is farther than this from the box, so an anchor on the edge does not load and unload it every tick; 0: 1.25 × load. Less than `load` is taken as `load`." {
      unit m
      min 0
    }
    field pin bool "Loaded whatever the anchors while true (a cell game code needs, such as a quest's destination); set it false and the anchors decide again."
    field loaded bool "Written by the engine: true while the sub-scene's entities are in the world (from the tick it loaded until the tick it unloaded)." {
      readonly true
    }
  }

  component StreamAnchor {
    doc "What loads cells: every entity with one (the player, a vehicle, each player of a networked game) loads the cells within their StreamCell.load of it, from its world position. Without any anchor the og.Camera resource's position anchors (refused in an og.net session)."
    field reach f32 "Added to every cell's load and unload distances for this anchor: a fast vehicle reaches further than a walker." {
      unit m
      min 0
    }
  }

  component CellMember {
    doc "Which cell an entity came from: every entity a cell's sub-scene spawns carries one, and when the cell unloads every entity naming it, with its descendants, is despawned, wherever it has moved. Remove it from an entity to keep it past its cell's unload (an item the player picked up); the cell spawns the item again from its sub-scene when it loads again."
    field cell Entity "The cell's entity." {
      readonly true
    }
  }

  event CellEvent {
    doc "A cell loaded or unloaded: sent in the tick it happened, before any system runs, so the tick's systems read it with the cell's entities already spawned (or despawned)."
    field cell Entity "The cell's entity."
    field loaded bool "True: its sub-scene's entities spawned; false: its members and their descendants were despawned."
    field entities u32 "How many entities spawned or were despawned."
  }

  enum StreamState {
    doc "Where a streaming group is."
    variant unloaded "Not wanted; its assets draw and play their stand-ins."
    variant queued "Wanted, waiting for room in the memory budget."
    variant loading "Admitted: its pack is being read (its memory already counts)."
    variant resident "Its assets are in place of their stand-ins."
    variant failed "Its pack could not be read, or it alone needs more than the budget (StreamStatus.reason says which)."
  }

  const STREAM_UNLOADED u32 0 "`StreamEvent.state`: the group is unloaded (StreamState unloaded)."
  const STREAM_QUEUED u32 1 "`StreamEvent.state`: the group waits for room in the budget (StreamState queued)."
  const STREAM_LOADING u32 2 "`StreamEvent.state`: the group's pack is being read (StreamState loading)."
  const STREAM_RESIDENT u32 3 "`StreamEvent.state`: the group's assets are in place (StreamState resident)."
  const STREAM_FAILED u32 4 "`StreamEvent.state`: the group failed (StreamState failed; og.stream.status says why)."

  event StreamEvent {
    doc "A group changed state: sent by og.stream at the start of the tick after the change."
    field group String "The group's name (at most 63 bytes)."
    field state u32 "Its new state: STREAM_UNLOADED, STREAM_QUEUED, STREAM_LOADING, STREAM_RESIDENT or STREAM_FAILED (StreamState's order)."
  }

  struct StreamStatus {
    doc "One streaming group."
    field name String "Its name."
    field scene String "The scene that declares it."
    field state StreamState "Where it is."
    field wanted bool "Whether its trigger or a request wants it now."
    field requested bool "Whether og.stream.request asked for it (until og.stream.release)."
    field priority i32 "Higher loads first and is evicted last."
    field trigger String "manual, distance or volume."
    field memory f64 "What its assets take once loaded, in bytes." {
      unit bytes
    }
    field bytes f64 "Its pack's size, in bytes." {
      unit bytes
    }
    field assets List.String "Its asset ids."
    field reason String "Why it is queued or failed; empty otherwise."
    field since f64 "The tick of its last change."
    field loads u32 "How many times it became resident."
    field kept f64 "The bytes of its assets kept for the frame after it unloaded (counted in StreamBudget.used until let go); 0 otherwise." {
      unit bytes
    }
  }

  struct StreamBudget {
    doc "The streaming memory budget and every group."
    field budget f64 "The bytes resident and loading groups may take." {
      unit bytes
    }
    field used f64 "The bytes they take now, with what unloaded groups keep for the frame." {
      unit bytes
    }
    field kept f64 "Of `used`, the bytes of assets kept for the frame after their group unloaded (an array a sprite still draws, the map the frame is lit by, ...)." {
      unit bytes
    }
    field peak f64 "The most they ever took (never above the budget at the time)." {
      unit bytes
    }
    field groups List.StreamStatus "Every group, by name."
  }

  fn status {
    doc "One streaming group's state."
    param world World "The world."
    param group String "The group's name, as its scene declares it."
    returns Result.StreamStatus "Refused for a group no scene declares."
  }

  fn request {
    doc "Ask for a group (any trigger) until og.stream.release: it loads from the next tick boundary, within the budget by its priority."
    param world World "The world."
    param group String "The group's name."
    returns Result.Void "Refused for a group no scene declares."
  }

  fn release {
    doc "Stop asking for a group; it unloads at the next tick boundary unless its trigger still wants it."
    param world World "The world."
    param group String "The group's name."
    returns Result.Void "Refused for a group no scene declares."
  }

  fn budget {
    doc "The memory budget, what the groups take now, the peak and every group."
    param world World "The world."
    returns Result.StreamBudget "The budget and the groups; refused only for a world that does not exist."
  }

  fn setBudget {
    doc "Set the memory budget from the next tick boundary; a lower one evicts the lowest-priority groups until the rest fit."
    param world World "The world."
    param bytes f64 "The bytes resident groups may take."
    returns Result.Void "Refused for a negative or non-finite size."
  }
}

